Bound the work of a mapping check. check_mapping() and check_mapping6() looked
up every tuple of the mapping on each call, and each new flow on the mapping
calls one under the global spinlock, so a flood of new inbound flows to a
mapped port cost O(n^2) conntrack lookups (lab: 60000 flows saturated both
CPUs and delayed new LAN flows by up to 2 s). Now the oldest tuples are taken
from the tail: dead ones are freed (each tuple once, paid for by its
insertion), live ones are rotated to the head, and the scan stops after 4 live
ones. A mapping is valid exactly when a live tuple is found, as before.

The IPv4 newrange is zeroed as a whole like the IPv6 one: base_proto was left
uninitialized (the kernel reads it only with NF_NAT_RANGE_PROTO_OFFSET).

--- a/xt_FULLCONENAT.c
+++ b/xt_FULLCONENAT.c
@@ -122,6 +122,13 @@
 };
 #endif
 
+/* live tuples looked up per mapping check. The old full scan made every new flow
+ * on a mapping cost one conntrack lookup per flow already on it, under the global
+ * lock: a flood to a mapped port was quadratic. With rotation every tuple is still
+ * revisited within refer_count / CHECK_MAPPING_LIVE_PROBES checks, so dead tuples
+ * stay bounded by the live ones. */
+#define CHECK_MAPPING_LIVE_PROBES 4
+
 struct tuple_list {
   struct nf_conntrack_tuple tuple_original;
   struct nf_conntrack_tuple tuple_reply;
